---
name: beam-campaign-node
description: Write a campaign node type for the Campaign Node Editor — a zone portal extension that sets how one Campaign System node kind is shown, edited and written, replacing the built-in one by priority.
---

# Campaign Node Types

> For the extension project itself (scaffold, build, deploy), load `beam-create-portal-extension`. For the campaign graph a node belongs to (node kinds, outcomes, publish, versions), load `beam-campaign-api`. A Send node's message comes from a rail (`beam-message-rail-federation`) and its offers from offer providers (`beam-offer-federation`).

## High-Level Architecture

```
Campaign Node Editor (zone extension, owner of the `campaign-node` site, presentation `host`)
  │  canvas · Add node menu · Inspector panel · working graph in the browser
  │
  ├─ per node kind, the `campaign-node` mount with the highest `priority` ──▶ node type extension
  │     describe({ config })                                    → lines, outputs, errors   (the editor draws the node)
  │     decode({ node })                                        → config                   (load)
  │     encode({ nodeId, campaignId, config, connectedOutputs }) → body, outcomes, pids      (save)
  │     Inspector: rendered by the editor for the selected node ──configChanged──▶ editor
  │
  └─ Save / Publish ──▶ POST /api/campaigns/draft | /api/campaigns/publish ──▶ Campaign System
                                                       (validates, versions, runs the graph)
```

## Code Mapping

| Part | File |
|---|---|
| Your node type: manifest (mount + static) | `package.json` → `beamable.mounts` |
| Your node type: contract (copied) | `src/campaignNode.ts` |
| Your node type: describe / decode / encode | `src/<kind>Node.ts` |
| Your node type: entrypoint | `src/main.tsx` |
| Your node type: Inspector | `src/App.tsx` |
| Your node type: round-trip test | `src/__tests__/<kind>Node.test.ts` |
| Site declaration (agentic-portal) | `bundles/features/player-engagement/portal-extensions/campaign-node-editor/package.json` → `beamable.sites` |
| Priority rule (agentic-portal) | `bundles/features/player-engagement/portal-extensions/campaign-node-editor/src/model/pickTypes.ts` |
| Graph written on Save (agentic-portal) | `bundles/features/player-engagement/portal-extensions/campaign-node-editor/src/model/save.ts` |
| Built-in node types (agentic-portal) | `bundles/extensions-libs/campaign-node/src/nodes/` (`entry`, `segment`, `schedule`, `gate`, `send`) |
| Built-in Schedule extension (agentic-portal) | `bundles/features/player-engagement/portal-extensions/campaign-node-schedule/` |

## Business Goals Constraints

- Operators author campaigns as a graph. Each Campaign System node kind — `Entry`, `Segment`, `Schedule`, `AnalyticsGate`, `Send` — is edited through one node type extension.
- A team changes how a kind is edited (its Inspector, its defaults, what the node shows) by shipping a node type with a higher `priority`. The editor needs no change.
- Behavior belongs to the Campaign System. A node type authors one of the five kinds and can only declare outcomes the runtime produces for it.
- One type per kind is active. A stored node records no type: the active type for its kind reads it.

## Performance Constraints

| Limit | Value | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Timeout per `describe` / `decode` / `encode` call | 10 000 ms | portal site channel |
| `describe` calls | one per node on load, one per config change | editor |
| `decode` calls | one per stored node on load; one per new node (an empty node of its kind) | editor |
| `encode` calls | one per edited or new node on Save / Publish | editor |
| `activate()` | once per kind (the active type), nothing mounted | editor |
| Inspector instances | one: the selected node's | editor |
| Nodes / edges per campaign | 500 / 2 000 | Campaign System |
| Goals / offers / offer groups per Send | 32 / 48 / 48 | Campaign System |
| `customProperties` per node | 64 entries; key ≤ 256 chars; value ≤ 65 536 chars | Campaign System |

## Usage Guide

### Steps

1. Create a **zone** portal extension in a Beamable workspace, like any portal extension (`beam-create-portal-extension`), with the project files below.
2. Add the mount to `package.json` (below). No `sites` declaration is needed: the editor owns `campaign-node`.
3. Copy the contract file to `src/campaignNode.ts`.
4. Write `src/<kind>Node.ts`: `describe`, `decode`, `encode`. Start from the Schedule reference.
5. Register the functions in `src/main.tsx`.
6. Write the Inspector in `src/App.tsx` with `useNodeConfig`.
7. Write the round-trip test against nodes as the built-in type writes them; run it with `vitest`.
8. Run the extension in a zone with the Campaign Node Editor: in the local stack, or deployed (`beam-build-and-deploy`). The Add node menu shows your type in place of the built-in one. A static, input, result or event that doesn't fit the site schema is reported as a site error in the browser console and in the extension's gear dialog.

### Project files

`package.json` (besides `beamable`, below):

```json
"name": "acme-schedule",
"private": true,
"version": "1.0.0",
"type": "module",
"scripts": { "build": "vite build", "test": "vitest run" },
"devDependencies": {
  "@beamable/portal-toolkit": "<the toolkit version of your other extensions>",
  "@types/react": "^19.0.0",
  "@types/react-dom": "^19.0.0",
  "@vitejs/plugin-react": "^4.3.4",
  "typescript": "^5.6.3",
  "vite": "^7.2.2",
  "vitest": "^3.2.4"
}
```

React comes from the portal at run time (the build marks it external), so it is a type-only dependency.

`vite.config.ts`:

```ts
import { definePortalExtensionConfig } from '@beamable/portal-toolkit/vite'
import { name } from './package.json'

export default definePortalExtensionConfig({ entry: 'src/main.tsx', name })
```

`tsconfig.json`:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ESNext",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "lib": ["ESNext", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "strict": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "types": ["vite/client"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.tsx"]
}
```

`resolveJsonModule` lets `main.tsx` and `vite.config.ts` read the extension name from `package.json`.

### Manifest

An override is the built-in type's mount for the same `kind` with a `priority` above 0 (the built-in types declare none):

```json
"beamable": {
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "portalExtension": true,
  "serviceScope": "zone",
  "mounts": [
    {
      "siteIds": ["campaign-node"],
      "static": {
        "kind": "Schedule",
        "title": "ACME Schedule",
        "icon": "clock",
        "category": "Flow",
        "description": "Waits for a time, waits until a date, or continues now.",
        "hasInput": true,
        "priority": 1
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

| `static` field | Type | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| `kind` | `"Entry"` \| `"Segment"` \| `"Schedule"` \| `"Send"` \| `"AnalyticsGate"` | Required |
| `title` | string, non-empty | Required. Shown in the Add node menu and on the node |
| `icon` | string | Required. Icon name, as in `navIcon` |
| `hasInput` | boolean | Required. `false` only for `Entry` |
| `category` | string | Optional |
| `description` | string, ≤ 140 chars | Optional. Shown under the title in the Add node menu |
| `priority` | integer | Optional; absent = 0. Built-in types declare none |

### Which type is active

| Case | Result |
|---|---|
| Several types for one kind | Candidates are ordered by extension name, then mount id (the portal derives one when the mount declares no `id`); the highest `priority` wins |
| Two or more at the highest priority | The first by extension name wins; the editor logs a warning and shows a toast |
| The winner is left out at load (`incompatible`) or its `activate()` fails | The next one of the kind is used; the editor logs a warning and shows a toast |
| No usable type for a kind | Nodes of that kind are read-only and saved unchanged |

### The site contract

| Part | Shape |
|---|---|
| Inspector `input` | `{ nodeId: string, config: object, connectedOutputs: string[] }` — `connectedOutputs` are the output keys that have an edge. The `ZoneExtensionContext` the Inspector receives carries `site` and `mount`, which `useSite` / `useNodeConfig` read |
| `describe` | args `{ config }` → `{ lines: string[] (≤ 3 shown, ≤ 80 chars each), outputs: { key, label, timing }[], errors: string[] }`. The first line is the node's title on the canvas; `errors` block Save |
| `decode` | args `{ node: GraphNodeDto }` → `{ config }`. Called with `{ node: { id, body: { kind } } }` for a new node |
| `encode` | args `{ nodeId, campaignId, config, connectedOutputs }` → `{ body: NodeBodyDto, outcomes: { key, timing }[], pids: string[] }` |
| `configChanged` event | payload `{ nodeId, config }` |
| `timing` | `"Immediate"` \| `"Delayed"` |

What the editor does with them:

- It draws each node from `describe`: the type's icon and title, the lines, one output port per `outputs` entry. An edge whose output is missing from the latest `describe` is removed.
- It writes `id`, `lane` and decorators itself. When `encode`'s `body` has `customProperties`, they replace the stored node's whole map; without it, the stored map is kept. The editor then adds `portalPos` (layout) and, on one Entry, `portalDescription`.
- It writes back a node nobody edited exactly as loaded, without calling `encode`.
- `acceptedRealms` is the union of every node's `pids` and every `targetPid` / `observePid` in the written bodies.

Rules for every type:

- `decode` reads any stored node of its kind, whatever wrote it, and never throws: an unreadable field becomes a default and `describe` reports it.
- `decode` followed by `encode`, with no edit in between, returns the same `body` JSON (`customProperties` key order included) and the same `outcomes`, for every node as the built-in type writes it (fields the kind doesn't define are not kept). A difference publishes as a structural change: a new campaign version, and the live one drains.
- Everything crossing the site is JSON. 64-bit fields (`*Ms`) are strings in a stored node and in `encode`'s output; instants are ISO strings.
- The three functions are pure and keep no state per node: one activated instance answers for every node of the kind, in parallel.
- Outcome keys must be ones the runtime produces for the kind (tables below). A declared outcome with no edge drops the player there.
- Authored event names may not start with `beam_`.

### Contract file

`src/campaignNode.ts`, copied as is:

```ts
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react'
import type { ExtensionMountPoint, ExtensionSite } from '@beamable/portal-toolkit'
import { useSite } from '@beamable/portal-toolkit/react'

export const CAMPAIGN_NODE_SITE = 'campaign-node'

export type NodeKind = 'Entry' | 'Segment' | 'Schedule' | 'Send' | 'AnalyticsGate'
export type OutcomeTiming = 'Immediate' | 'Delayed'

export interface NodeOutput { key: string; label: string; timing: OutcomeTiming }

export interface NodeStatic {
  kind: NodeKind
  title: string
  icon: string
  category?: string
  description?: string
  hasInput: boolean
  priority?: number
}

export interface NodeInput<C> { nodeId: string; config: C; connectedOutputs: string[] }
export interface DescribeResult { lines: string[]; outputs: NodeOutput[]; errors: string[] }

/** A stored node body, as JSON: `{ kind, <kind>: { ... }, customProperties }`. */
export type WireBody = { kind?: string; customProperties?: Record<string, string> | null; [field: string]: unknown }
export interface WireNode { id?: string; lane?: string | null; body?: WireBody; outcomes?: { key?: string; timing?: string }[] }

export interface EncodeArgs<C> { nodeId: string; campaignId: string; config: C; connectedOutputs: string[] }
export interface EncodeResult { body: WireBody; outcomes: { key: string; timing: OutcomeTiming }[]; pids: string[] }

export interface NodeDefinition<C> {
  describe(args: { config: C }): DescribeResult
  decode(args: { node: WireNode }): { config: C }
  encode(args: EncodeArgs<C>): EncodeResult
}

/** The `functions` option of `registerReactZoneExtension`. */
export function siteFunctions<C>(def: NodeDefinition<C>) {
  return {
    [CAMPAIGN_NODE_SITE]: {
      describe: async (args: { config: C }) => def.describe(args),
      decode: async (args: { node: WireNode }) => def.decode(args),
      encode: async (args: EncodeArgs<C>) => def.encode(args),
    },
  }
}

/**
 * The selected node's config, for its Inspector. Kept locally so typing never waits on the editor; each
 * change is emitted as `configChanged`, addressed by node id. A value equal to the current one is not
 * emitted (some inputs re-report the value they were given).
 */
export function useNodeConfig<C>(context: { site: ExtensionSite; mount: ExtensionMountPoint }) {
  const { input, emit } = useSite<unknown, NodeInput<C>>(context, CAMPAIGN_NODE_SITE)
  const [config, setConfig] = useState<C>(input.config)
  const nodeRef = useRef(input.nodeId)
  const configRef = useRef(config)
  configRef.current = config

  useEffect(() => {
    if (input.nodeId !== nodeRef.current) {
      nodeRef.current = input.nodeId
      setConfig(input.config)
    }
  }, [input.nodeId, input.config])

  const update = (next: C | ((current: C) => C)) => {
    const value = typeof next === 'function' ? (next as (current: C) => C)(configRef.current) : next
    if (JSON.stringify(value) === JSON.stringify(configRef.current)) return
    configRef.current = value
    setConfig(value)
    emit('configChanged', { nodeId: nodeRef.current, config: value })
  }

  return { nodeId: input.nodeId, config, update, connectedOutputs: input.connectedOutputs }
}

/** A stored duration (`string | null`) as milliseconds, or null. */
export function msFrom(value: unknown): number | null {
  if (value === null || value === undefined || value === '') return null
  const n = Number(value)
  return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null
}

/** Milliseconds as the platform's 64-bit string, or null. */
export function msTo(value: number | null | undefined): string | null {
  return value === null || value === undefined ? null : String(Math.round(value))
}
```

### Reference: Schedule

`src/scheduleNode.ts`:

```ts
import { msFrom, msTo, type NodeDefinition } from './campaignNode'

export type ScheduleMode = 'for' | 'until' | 'now'
export interface ScheduleConfig { mode: ScheduleMode; delayMs: number | null; when: string | null }

const DAY_MS = 86_400_000

function duration(ms: number): string {
  for (const [unit, size] of [['day', DAY_MS], ['hour', 3_600_000], ['minute', 60_000]] as const) {
    if (ms >= size && ms % size === 0) return `${ms / size} ${unit}${ms / size === 1 ? '' : 's'}`
  }
  return `${Math.round(ms / 60_000)} minutes`
}

export const scheduleNode: NodeDefinition<ScheduleConfig> = {
  describe({ config }) {
    const errors: string[] = []
    let line: string
    if (config.mode === 'for') {
      line = `Wait ${config.delayMs ? duration(config.delayMs) : '—'}`
      if (!(config.delayMs && config.delayMs > 0)) errors.push('Set how long to wait.')
    } else if (config.mode === 'until') {
      const when = config.when ? new Date(config.when) : null
      const valid = !!when && !Number.isNaN(when.getTime())
      line = `Wait until ${valid ? when!.toLocaleString() : '—'}`
      if (!valid) errors.push('Pick the date and time to wait until.')
    } else {
      line = 'Continue now'
    }
    return {
      lines: [line],
      outputs: config.mode === 'now'
        ? [{ key: 'Done', label: 'Then', timing: 'Immediate' }]
        : [{ key: 'Elapsed', label: 'Elapsed', timing: 'Delayed' }],
      errors,
    }
  },

  decode({ node }) {
    const body = (node.body?.schedule ?? {}) as { kind?: string; delayMs?: unknown; when?: string | null }
    const mode: ScheduleMode = body.kind === 'AtDateTime' ? 'until' : body.kind === 'SendNow' ? 'now' : 'for'
    // An empty node (a new one) starts as a one-day wait; a stored one is read as it is.
    const delayMs = body.kind ? msFrom(body.delayMs) : DAY_MS
    return { config: { mode, delayMs, when: body.when ?? null } }
  },

  encode({ config }) {
    const schedule =
      config.mode === 'for' ? { kind: 'DelayFor', delayMs: msTo(config.delayMs) }
      : config.mode === 'until' ? { kind: 'AtDateTime', when: config.when }
      : { kind: 'SendNow' }
    return {
      body: { kind: 'Schedule', schedule },
      outcomes: config.mode === 'now' ? [{ key: 'Done', timing: 'Immediate' }] : [{ key: 'Elapsed', timing: 'Delayed' }],
      pids: [],
    }
  },
}
```

`src/main.tsx`:

```tsx
import { registerReactZoneExtension } from '@beamable/portal-toolkit/react'
import { name as beamId } from '../package.json'
import App from './App'
import { siteFunctions } from './campaignNode'
import { scheduleNode } from './scheduleNode'

registerReactZoneExtension({ beamId, App, functions: siteFunctions(scheduleNode) })
```

`src/App.tsx` (the Inspector):

```tsx
import type { ZoneExtensionContext } from '@beamable/portal-toolkit'
import { BeamInput, BeamOption, BeamSelect } from '@beamable/portal-toolkit/react'
import { useNodeConfig } from './campaignNode'
import { scheduleNode, type ScheduleConfig, type ScheduleMode } from './scheduleNode'

const UNITS = [['minutes', 60_000], ['hours', 3_600_000], ['days', 86_400_000]] as const
const column = { display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: 8 } as const
const inline = { display: 'flex', gap: 8 } as const

/** The largest unit that divides a duration exactly, so any stored value shows (and edits) without rounding. */
function unitOf(ms: number): number {
  return [...UNITS].reverse().find(([, size]) => ms % size === 0)?.[1] ?? 60_000
}

/** `datetime-local` wants local "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"; the config keeps an ISO instant. */
function toLocalInput(iso: string | null): string {
  const d = iso ? new Date(iso) : null
  if (!d || Number.isNaN(d.getTime())) return ''
  const pad = (n: number) => String(n).padStart(2, '0')
  return `${d.getFullYear()}-${pad(d.getMonth() + 1)}-${pad(d.getDate())}T${pad(d.getHours())}:${pad(d.getMinutes())}`
}

export default function App({ context }: { context: ZoneExtensionContext }) {
  const { config, update } = useNodeConfig<ScheduleConfig>(context)
  const set = (patch: Partial<ScheduleConfig>) => update((c) => ({ ...c, ...patch }))
  const errors = scheduleNode.describe({ config }).errors
  const delayMs = config.delayMs && config.delayMs > 0 ? config.delayMs : 86_400_000
  const unit = unitOf(delayMs)

  return (
    <div style={column}>
      <label style={column}>
        This step
        <BeamSelect size="small" value={config.mode} onValueChange={(v: string) => set({ mode: v as ScheduleMode })}>
          <BeamOption value="for">Wait for a duration</BeamOption>
          <BeamOption value="until">Wait until a date and time</BeamOption>
          <BeamOption value="now">Continue now</BeamOption>
        </BeamSelect>
      </label>
      {config.mode === 'for' && (
        <div style={inline}>
          <BeamInput
            size="small"
            type="number"
            value={String(delayMs / unit)}
            onValueInput={(v: string) => set({ delayMs: Math.max(1, Math.round(Number(v) || 1)) * unit })}
          />
          <BeamSelect size="small" value={String(unit)} onValueChange={(v: string) => set({ delayMs: (delayMs / unit) * Number(v) })}>
            {UNITS.map(([label, size]) => (
              <BeamOption key={label} value={String(size)}>
                {label}
              </BeamOption>
            ))}
          </BeamSelect>
        </div>
      )}
      {config.mode === 'until' && (
        <label style={column}>
          Until (local time)
          <BeamInput
            size="small"
            type="datetime-local"
            value={toLocalInput(config.when)}
            onValueInput={(v: string) => set({ when: v ? new Date(v).toISOString() : null })}
          />
        </label>
      )}
      {errors.map((e) => (
        <span key={e}>{e}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

`src/__tests__/scheduleNode.test.ts` — the three nodes below are exactly what the built-in Schedule writes, one per mode:

```ts
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest'
import type { WireNode } from '../campaignNode'
import { scheduleNode } from '../scheduleNode'

const stored: WireNode[] = [
  { id: 'wait', body: { kind: 'Schedule', schedule: { kind: 'DelayFor', delayMs: '86400000' } }, outcomes: [{ key: 'Elapsed', timing: 'Delayed' }] },
  { id: 'until', body: { kind: 'Schedule', schedule: { kind: 'AtDateTime', when: '2026-12-01T13:00:00.000Z' } }, outcomes: [{ key: 'Elapsed', timing: 'Delayed' }] },
  { id: 'done', body: { kind: 'Schedule', schedule: { kind: 'SendNow' } }, outcomes: [{ key: 'Done', timing: 'Immediate' }] },
]

describe('Schedule', () => {
  it.each(stored.map((n) => [n.id!, n] as const))('decode then encode gives back %s', (_id, node) => {
    const { config } = scheduleNode.decode({ node })
    const out = scheduleNode.encode({ nodeId: node.id!, campaignId: 'cmp', config: JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(config)), connectedOutputs: [] })
    expect(out.body).toEqual(node.body)
    expect(out.outcomes).toEqual(node.outcomes)
  })

  it('outputs follow the mode', () => {
    const outputs = (mode: 'for' | 'until' | 'now') => scheduleNode.describe({ config: { mode, delayMs: 60_000, when: null } }).outputs.map((o) => o.key)
    expect(outputs('now')).toEqual(['Done'])
    expect(outputs('for')).toEqual(['Elapsed'])
  })
})
```

### Wire reference: Entry

| | |
|---|---|
| `body` | `{ kind: "Entry", entry: { trigger, requalify } }` |
| `trigger` | `{ kind: "AutoSegment", segment }` \| `{ kind: "Event", event }` \| `{ kind: "Inaction", event, windowMs }` — only the fields of its kind |
| `requalify` | `{ kind: "Never" }` \| `{ kind: "Immediately" }` \| `{ kind: "After", cooldownMs }` |
| `outcomes` | `[{ key: "Enrolled", timing: "Immediate" }]` |
| `static.hasInput` | `false` |
| `customProperties` | Don't return any: the editor keeps the stored ones (one Entry carries `portalDescription`) |
| Validation | `segment` is a zone segment id; `windowMs` and `cooldownMs` > 0; `event` not `beam_*`; one `AutoSegment` entry per lane (the editor gives each Entry its own lane) |
| `pids` | `[]` |

### Wire reference: Segment

| | |
|---|---|
| `body` | `{ kind: "Segment", segment: { segment, evaluationWindowMs } }` — `evaluationWindowMs` is a string or `null` |
| `outcomes` | `[{ key: "Yes", timing: "Immediate" }, { key: "No", timing: "Immediate" }]` — fixed keys |
| `pids` | `[]` |

### Wire reference: AnalyticsGate

| | |
|---|---|
| `body` | `{ kind: "AnalyticsGate", analyticsGate: { filter, observePid, maxWaitMs } }` — `maxWaitMs` string or `null` (wait indefinitely) |
| `filter` | One leaf `{ kind: "EventOccurred", event, properties?, propertiesMode?, observePid? }`, or `{ kind: "AllOf" \| "AnyOf", predicates: [≥ 2 leaves] }`. No `EventAbsent`, no `withinMs` in a gate. `propertiesMode` only when `"Any"`; `properties` only when non-empty |
| `observePid` | The realm the filter watches; a leaf's own `observePid` overrides it |
| `outcomes` | `[{ key: "Met", timing: "Delayed" }]`, plus `{ key: "NotMet", timing: "Delayed" }` only when `maxWaitMs` is set |
| `pids` | `observePid` and every leaf `observePid` |

### Wire reference: Send

| | |
|---|---|
| `body` | `{ kind: "Send", send: { railFederationId, targetPid, message, campaignOffers, campaignOfferGroups, goals, maxWaitMs, offerDelivery?, offerMaxGrantWaitMs?, deliveryMaxWaitMs? }, customProperties }` |
| `message` | `` `${campaignId}:${railFederationId}` `` |
| `targetPid` | Required: the realm the message goes to and offers are granted in |
| `goals` | `[{ label, predicate, observePid, timerMs: null }]` — labels unique per node, no `.`, no leading `$`; events not `beam_*` |
| `maxWaitMs` | The conversion window, > 0 and ≥ every leaf `withinMs`. The built-in Send writes the larger of 604 800 000 and the longest leaf window |
| `campaignOffers` | `[{ entryId, offerId, federationId, label, invalidatesEntryIds \| null, condition?, extraData \| null, durationMs \| null, required }]`; `[]` with none |
| `campaignOfferGroups` | `[{ id, label \| null, memberEntryIds, mode: "All" \| "Exclusive" }]` or `null` |
| `offerDelivery`, `offerMaxGrantWaitMs` | Only when the node has offers. `"AllOrNothing"` \| `"AtLeastOne"` \| `"BestEffort"` |
| `customProperties` | The rail's `extraData` keys (none starting with `portal`), then `portalRailBeamId`, `portalLane`, `portalLaneName`, `portalGoalNames` (JSON goal id → name, only when a goal is renamed), then any other stored `portal*` key — in this order. The rail reads the whole map |
| `pids` | `targetPid` and every goal `observePid` |

| Outcome | Timing | Declared |
|---|---|---|
| `NoGoal` — no goal met when the window closes | `Delayed` | Always |
| `beam_met_{goal label}` — a goal met; the player moves at once | `Immediate` | Only when connected |
| `Sent`, `Delivered`, `Opened`, `Clicked` — the deepest stage reached when the window closes | `Delayed` | Only when connected |
| `NotSent` — the rail never acknowledged the send | `Delayed` | Only when connected |
| `Skipped` — no player in `targetPid` | `Immediate` | Only when connected |
| `OffersNotGranted` — offers failed under `offerDelivery` | `Immediate` | Only when connected |

A Send Inspector that keeps the message and offer pickers renders the `message-rail` and `campaign-offer` sites, so the extension declares them in `beamable.sites` with the Campaigns extension's declarations, passes `{ laneId: nodeId, laneName, realmPid: targetPid, draft }` as their input, and applies their `draftChanged` events to the node's config.
